-- EXPECT-ERROR: does not satisfy its return type constraint on all code paths
-- Refinement types (widening slice 12, §2.14). A function declared with a
-- refined RETURN type has its OWN BODY checked -- once, at the function's
-- definition site, independent of any caller -- to prove every path produces
-- a value satisfying the predicate. `bad`'s `n < 0` branch returns `n` itself
-- (negative by the guard), directly violating `_ >= 0`; Z3 returns a concrete
-- counterexample (`n = -1`) that `--check` surfaces in the diagnostic. Accept
-- companion: accept/t76 (an exhaustive guard that DOES satisfy every branch).
mod M do
  fn bad(n : Int) : {Int | _ >= 0} do
    if n < 0 do n else 0 end
  end
  fn main() do
    println(int_to_string(bad(-3)))
  end
end
